'''transductor''' (trȧns-duk′tor), ''n.'' [NL., < L. ''transducere'', pp. ''transductus'', lead over: see ''traduce.''] In ''anat.'', that which draws across: specifying a muscle of the great toe.—'''Transductor hallucis''', {{smaller|a transverse muscle of the sole of the human foot, acting upon the great toe; the transversus pedis.}}

'''transe†.''' An obsolete spelling of ''trance''<sup>1</sup>, ''trance''<sup>2</sup>.

'''transearth†''' (trȧns-ėrth′), ''v. t.'' [< ''trans-'' + ''earth''<sup>1</sup>.] To transplant.

{{smaller block|Fruits of hotter countries ''transearth'd'' in colder climates have vigour enough in themselves to be fructuous according to their nature.
{{right|''Feltham'', Resolves, i. 19.}}}}

'''transect''' (trȧn-sekt′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''trans'', across, + ''secare'', pp. ''sectus'', cut: see ''section.''] To cut across; dissect transversely.

{{smaller block|The meshes of the dotted substance, as described by other authors, are only the ''transected'' sheaths of the tubules.
{{right|''Amer. Jour. Psychol.'', I. 488.}}}}

'''transection<sup>1</sup>''' (trȧn-sek′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''transect'' + ''-ion.''] In ''anat.'', the dissection of a body transversely; transverse section: correlated with ''longisection''. ''Wilder'', N. Y. Med. Jour., Aug. 2, 1884, p. 114.

'''transection<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' See ''transexion.''

'''transelement†''' (trȧns-el′ē-ment), ''v. t.'' [< ''trans-'' + ''element.''] To change or transform the elements of.

{{smaller block|For, as he saith wee are ''transelemented'', or trans-natured, and changed into Christe, euen so, and none otherwise, wee saie, the breade is ''transelemented'', or changed into Christes body.
{{right|''Bp. Jewell'', Reply to Harding, p. 238.}}}}

'''transelementate†''' (trȧns-el-ē-men′tāt), ''v. t.'' [< ''trans-'' + ''element'' + ''-ate''<sup>2</sup>.] Same as ''transele-''

