'''transept-aisle''' (tran′sept-īl), ''n.'' An aisle of a transept where, as is commonly the case in cathedrals and large medieval churches, the transept is divided, like the body of the church, into nave and aisles. See plan under ''cathedral.''

{{smaller block|Where there are no ''transept aisles'', as in the east transept of Lincoln, there are, of course, no vertical divisions in the façade [end of transept].
{{right|''C. H. Moore'', Gothic Architecture, p. 160.}}}}

'''transeptal''' (tran-sep′tal), ''a.'' [< ''transept'' + ''-al.''] Of or pertaining to a transept.

{{smaller block|''Transeptal'' towers occur elsewhere in England only in the collegiate church of Ottery, in Devonshire, where the cathedral served as a model.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', VIII. 802.}}}}

'''transeunt''' (trȧn′sē-unt), ''a.'' [< L. ''trans'', over, + ''eun(t-)s'', ppr. of ''ire'', go. Cf. ''transient.''] Passing outward; operating outside of itself: opposed to ''immanent.''

{{smaller block|The functions of the subject or psyche . . . may be exhaustively divided into (1) sense-presentation. . . . (3) volitionally reactive redintegration, with its two stages, immanent and ''transeunt'' action. ''Athenæum'', No. 3289, p. 631.}}

'''transexion†''' (trȧn-sek′shọn), ''n.'' [Erroneously ''transection''; < ''trans-'' + ''sex'' + ''-ion.''] Transformation as regards sex; change of sex.

{{smaller block|It much impeacheth this iterated ''transection'' of hares if}}

